WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:04.270
So when we think of Africa, we often think
of it as one place, right?

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:08.350
You know, it's this poor, disease-ridden continent
where people live in thatched-roof huts and

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:11.780
children don't get good educations, and then
there's like a tiny wealthy elite, most of

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.889
whom wear overly decorous military uniforms,
smoke cigars, and laugh about all of the aid

00:00:15.889 --> 00:00:16.920
that they're embezzling.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.060
But yeah, no.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.550
For one thing, Africa isn't just one place.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.880
Most Canadians live closer to Venezuela than
Liberians live to Botswana.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.900
But for another, those stereotypes are just
ridiculous.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.430
I mean, I did meet a lot of people who live
in thatched-roof huts in Ethiopia and a lot

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:34.350
of children who didn't have access to good
schools, and corruption remains a significant

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:39.820
problem, BUT just as Ethiopia's poverty rate
is falling, its middle class is rising.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.730
More and more kids go to primary school, and
the main university in Addis Ababa which in

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:48.090
1950 had thirty-two students today has more
than 42,000.

00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:50.990
And when I was in Ethiopia I got to meet a
bunch of them with Bill Gates including this

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:54.160
young woman, Meheret, who started an anti-domestic
violence initiative.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.370
[Meheret:] Basically a group of volunteers
that, uh, that want to see a better community

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:03.770
and that want to see a better country and
a better society so we bas—so we work on

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:04.770
gender and race violence.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:07.850
[John:] So yeah, Ethiopia has anti-domestic
violence activists and insurance agents and

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.399
web developers and nurses and etcetera.

00:01:10.399 --> 00:01:13.209
Okay Hank, I want to give you a sense of some
of these students, like these two young women

00:01:13.209 --> 00:01:16.619
are pre-med, and they talked a lot about the
risks and opportunities of working abroad.

00:01:16.619 --> 00:01:19.959
[Pre-med student:] There are so many brilliant
minds going to the Western countries and,

00:01:19.959 --> 00:01:25.159
you know, they won't come back.

00:01:25.159 --> 00:01:28.969
[John:] The other students were future engineers
and lawyers and a couple people who weren't

00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:30.240
sure what they wanted to do yet.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.450
You know, like me in college.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.329
I waited until like three minutes into the
conversation before I asked if they watched

00:01:35.329 --> 00:01:36.329
Youtube.

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:37.929
[John:] Do—do you watch videos on Youtube?

00:01:37.929 --> 00:01:39.170
[Student:] Yeah.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:40.170
[John:] For fun, or for school?

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.579
[Student:] For fun and for school.

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:45.649
[John:] Hank, for fun AND for school?!

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:48.130
These people are nerdfighters if ever I have
seen one.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:51.369
But this brings up an interesting problem,
which is that the internet in Ethiopia is

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:52.719
CRAZY SLOW.

00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:55.529
[Dial-up modem noise plays] I mean, like,
yes, that slow.

00:01:55.529 --> 00:01:58.829
That makes it hard to watch a lot of creators,
it makes it hard to comment; it also takes

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:00.380
forever for Tumblr to load.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:04.600
And we talked about how that makes it hard
to be an active participant in online communities.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:08.539
Almost all of their social media interaction
happens with people they know in real life.

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:10.280
To which I said, "What is real life??"

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:12.439
It was also interesting to talk to them about
platforms.

00:02:12.439 --> 00:02:15.480
Like, I asked this future engineer what what
social media he uses…

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.420
[Future engineer:] Facebook not as much now.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.090
I'm more going to Twitter now.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.200
[John:] That's right, Hank.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.090
College students all over the world are so
over Facebook.

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:26.650
Also, just like I hear from a lot of American
students, these students felt a lot of tension

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.770
between, like, what they dreamt of doing and
what they'd been told they should do.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:34.099
Although in their case it wasn't just about
parental wishes or future job opportunities,

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:35.769
there was also a kind of larger sense of responsibility.

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:39.720
[Future engineer:] Something is expected from
you in terms of what your country really needs

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:40.720
now.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.900
[John:] And I also heard a lot of concern,
which I'm sure some nerdfighters can relate

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:47.599
to, that book-learning might not translate
to real-world success.

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:53.239
[Student:] You know, I got A+ on different
subjects.

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:55.440
I know how to do integrations.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.440
But how can I just express that in reality?

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.930
[John:] Hank I think we have to pause quickly
here just to note the astonishing zit on the

00:02:58.930 --> 00:02:59.959
side of my neck.

00:02:59.959 --> 00:03:01.390
Are you sentient, zit?

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:02.659
Do you have a soul?

00:03:02.659 --> 00:03:05.840
Anyway, so after meeting with these students,
I went to the library's bathroom, where there

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.420
was a bucket full of water that you had to
pour into the tank in order to make the toilet

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:12.999
flush because there is no running water in
the library.

00:03:12.999 --> 00:03:14.760
Hank, there isn't just one Ethiopia.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:19.879
The college library with no water is Ethiopia,
but these future engineers who will build

00:03:19.879 --> 00:03:22.609
the city's infrastructure are also Ethiopia.

00:03:22.609 --> 00:03:28.140
These kids are Ethiopia, and so is this doctor,
and so are these farmers, and so is this presidential

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.140
marching band.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.769
Now I know that when we see Africa on the
news or in the movies it's always like "War!

00:03:32.769 --> 00:03:33.769
Pirates!

00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:34.769
Refugees!

00:03:34.769 --> 00:03:35.769
Disease!"

00:03:35.769 --> 00:03:39.680
But the stories I saw in Ethiopia were far
more complicated and ultimately more compelling.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.189
These remarkable young people have seen the
greatest drops in poverty rates and infant

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:46.939
mortality in their country's history, but
every year in Ethiopia, tens of thousands

00:03:46.939 --> 00:03:49.790
of children still die of easily-preventable
diseases.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:53.139
And if we've learned one thing from Global
health in the last twenty years, it's that

00:03:53.139 --> 00:03:58.230
if those kids don't die, and they don't have
to, then they can grow up and end up studying

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.230
in that library.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:03:59.880
I'll see you on Friday.

